The rewrite calls `GetType()` once instead of twice. It also reads the bytes through a raw pointer to `values`. When the field's byte size equals `sizeof(T)` on a little-endian host, it does one `memcpy` instead of the byte-by-byte shift loop. That gives the same bits as the original loop, because the loop assembles bytes least-significant first. Mismatched sizes and big-endian hosts keep the original loop unchanged.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T FieldBase::GetValue(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex) const
{
    const FIT_UINT8 typeNum = GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK;
    const FIT_UINT8 size = baseTypeSizes[typeNum];
    if (fieldArrayIndex >= (FIT_UINT8)values.size())
    {
        return *reinterpret_cast<const T*>(baseTypeInvalids[typeNum]);
    }

    const auto* src = &values[0] + (static_cast<size_t>(fieldArrayIndex) * size);

#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && defined(__ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
    if (size == sizeof(T))
    {
        T rv;
        std::memcpy(&rv, src, sizeof(T));
        return rv;
    }
#endif

    T rv = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        rv |= static_cast<T>(src[i]) << (i * 8);
    }

    return rv;
}
```

